p(24,4,"York came to me first, while the groom stood at Ginger’s head",
 "York pulls the rein tight. Ginger rears, kicks and falls over the carriage pole, and York sits on her head. Beauty is freed and led to his box, angry and sore.",
 "York came to me first, while the groom stood at Ginger’s head. He pulled my head back and fixed the rein so tight that it was almost too much to bear. Then he went to Ginger, who was jerking her head up and down against the bit in a hurry, as she did now. She had a good idea of what was coming. The moment York took the rein off the terret to make it shorter, she used her chance and reared up so suddenly that York’s nose was hit hard and his hat was knocked off. The groom was nearly thrown off his feet. At once they both ran to her head. But she was strong enough for them, and went on plunging, rearing and kicking in a wild way. At last she kicked right over the carriage pole and fell down, after giving me a hard blow on my near quarter. No one can know what more harm she might have done if York had not quickly sat down flat on her head to stop her struggling. At the same time he called out, “Unbuckle the black horse! Run for the winch and unscrew the carriage pole! Cut the trace here, somebody, if you can’t unhitch it!” One of the footmen ran for the winch, and another brought a knife from the house. The groom soon set me free from Ginger and the carriage and led me to my box. He just put me in as I was and ran back to York. What had happened excited me very much. If I had ever been used to kick or rear, I am sure I would have done it then. But I never had. So there I stood, angry, with a sore leg, my head still pulled up to the terret on the saddle, and no power to get it down. I was very unhappy and wanted very much to kick the first person who came near me.",
 ["intolerable","terret","reared","carriage pole","promptly","winch","unscrew","trace","unhitch","strained"])
